---
import Solution from '../../layouts/Solution.astro';
import SolutionExample from '../../components/SolutionExample.astro';
import SolutionStarters from '../../components/SolutionStarters.astro';
import SolutionFit from '../../components/SolutionFit.astro';
import SolutionFaq from '../../components/SolutionFaq.astro';
import DocsHandoff from '../../components/DocsHandoff.astro';
import { solutionAreas, sharedFamilyFaq } from '../../data/solutions';
import { askQuestionHref, contactEmail } from '../../data/contact';
const area = solutionAreas.find((a) => a.key === 'reference-registers')!;
---
<Solution
  area={area}
  heading="Publish the lists other registers rely on, from one maintained source."
  description="Other registers and services keep their own copies of your codes and names, so when your office renames a territory or withdraws a tax regime, each copy drifts out of date on its own schedule. Registry Stack gives your office one maintained list that every register reads from, with the history of each entry."
>
  <SolutionExample
    eyebrow="One example: official codes for administrative areas"
    heading="Give every register the same list of administrative areas."
    intro="An address register and a statistics office both need the official code and name of each administrative area. Today each keeps its own copy, updated from a spreadsheet sent by email."
    items={[
      { term: 'Who asks', body: 'The address register team and the statistics office, each for the areas it uses.' },
      { term: 'What the office wants', body: 'One list with each area’s code, level, parent area, and the dates it applies, so neither team keeps its own copy.' },
      { term: 'What happens', body: 'Your office keeps the list in its register. Each service gets the details it has agreed with you and reads the current list instead of waiting for the next spreadsheet.' },
      { term: 'What stays with your office', body: 'Your office decides when a name or boundary changes. The boundary maps stay in the mapping system the list refers to.' },
    ]}
    link={{ href: '/products/registry-relay/', label: 'See how Registry Relay shares agreed details' }}
  />
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="reference-change-title">
    <div class="section-inner">
      <div class="section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">What changes for your office</p>
        <h2 id="reference-change-title">One list to maintain, and no copies drifting apart.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="product-facts">
        <article><h3>Every register uses the same code for the same place</h3><p>Each entry carries its official code, its parent where there is one, and the dates it applies. The address register and the statistics office read the same list, so a district has one code and one name in both.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Only your team changes the list</h3><p>Your staff record every change, whether a new district, a renamed ministry, or a withdrawn tax regime. Other teams read the entries your office lets them see; the directory of public bodies is kept by your staff, not edited by each body listed in it.</p></article>
        <article><h3>A renamed territory keeps its old entry</h3><p>When a territory is renamed or a tax regime withdrawn, the earlier entry stays, with the dates it applied. A record that pointed to the old name years ago still makes sense today.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <SolutionStarters
    area={area}
    heading="Start from a reference register like yours."
    intro="A starter sets out what a list of this kind holds and who may change it, for one common directory, code list, or catalogue. Your team takes the closest one and adapts it to the list it maintains."
  />
  <SolutionFit
    area={area}
    heading="Give other registers one source to refer to."
    jobs={{
      'base-registry': 'Holds the list and the history of every entry, under your rules for who may record a change.',
      'protected-registry-apis': 'Gives each register and service that uses the list the details it has agreed with your office, always current.',
    }}
    extra={[
      { term: 'Other registers refer to the list by code', body: 'Registers of businesses, land, licences, and people refer to territories, public bodies, and products by code. A maintained list gives those codes one source.' },
      { term: 'Your office keeps deciding', body: 'The decision to create a district, rename a ministry, or change a tax regime is taken where it is taken today. The list records the result once it is decided.' },
    ]}
    muted
  >
    <p>Each product runs on its own. An office that already keeps its list in a database can start by sharing it through Registry Relay, and move the list into the Base Registry Engine later.</p>
  </SolutionFit>
  <SolutionFaq
    items={[
      {
        q: 'Does this replace the spreadsheet we send out?',
        a: 'It replaces the copies. Your team still maintains the list, in the register instead of a spreadsheet, and each service reads the current version from it instead of keeping its own.',
        link: { href: 'https://docs.registrystack.org/explanation/integration-patterns/', label: 'How it sits beside existing systems' },
      },
      {
        q: 'What happens to records that point to an old code?',
        a: 'They keep working. A renamed or withdrawn entry stays in the list with the dates it applied, so a record that used it still points to a real entry, and anyone reading it can see when it stopped applying.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Who can change the list?',
        a: 'Only the people your office names. Your office decides who may record a new entry, who may close one, and who may only read; every change is kept with who made it and when.',
        link: { href: '/security/', label: 'Who is responsible for what' },
      },
      {
        q: 'What is a starter, exactly?',
        a: 'A free package requested from the starter’s page and opened to see how a register of this kind is set out: the records it keeps, the changes staff can make, and who may make them. It is a starting point to adapt, not a running system: your team adds what your register needs, and using one does not mean the register is in use anywhere. Anyone from the office can request it; your technical team is who opens it.',
        link: { href: '/starters/#reference-registers', label: 'Browse the reference register starters' },
      },
      {
        q: 'Where does our technical team start?',
        a: 'With the Base Registry Engine tutorial, which builds a small register on a laptop, and then with the starter closest to your register.',
        link: { href: 'https://docs.registrystack.org/tutorials/first-breg/', label: 'Run the registry tutorial' },
      },
      ...sharedFamilyFaq,
    ]}
  />
  <DocsHandoff
    eyebrow="Work with us"
    heading="Talk it through with the people who build it."
    body="Tell us which register you keep and what other services ask of it. We reply by email, and a short call is the usual next step. Paid pilots and support are there if you want them."
    primaryLabel="Ask a question"
    primaryHref={askQuestionHref(area.name)}
    emphasis="docs"
    note={`Or write to ${contactEmail}.`}
  />
</Solution>
